1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,400
Right, let me show you the three knots that actually hold under load.

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:11,200
The first one is the bowline, and it is the one most people tie wrong.

3
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:17,000
You want the working end coming out of the loop on the inside, not the outside.

4
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:22,800
Tied inside out it still looks correct, and it fails at about half the load.

5
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:28,600
So check that every single time. Inside. Always inside.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,800
Now let me get the rope out and I will show you the second one.

7
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:46,900
Okay. This is a clove hitch, and it is the fastest knot on this list.

8
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:52,700
Two turns, tuck the end under the second turn, and pull it tight.

9
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:58,500
It holds beautifully under steady tension and it slips under a shock load.

10
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:04,300
So use it to hang something, never to catch something. That distinction matters.

11
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:09,500
Let me just find the thicker rope for the last one, hang on.

12
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,400
Here we go. The third knot is the alpine butterfly and it is the useful one.

13
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:31,200
It puts a fixed loop in the middle of a rope without touching either end.

14
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:37,000
Which means you can isolate a damaged section without cutting the rope.

15
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:42,800
It also takes load from all three directions, and almost nothing else does.

16
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:48,600
Wrap twice around your hand, pull the back strand over the front, and through.

17
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,400
That is the whole trick. Bowline to end, clove hitch to hang, butterfly to fix.

18
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:59,600
Practice those three and you can stop learning knots entirely.
